\section*{Introction}
The Gaussian integral stands as one of the most elegant and powerful mathematical tools in modern analysis. Named after the prolific mathematician Carl Friedrich Gauss, this integral forms the cornerstone of numerous disciplines including probability theory, statistical mechanics, quantum field theory, and signal processing. In its most fundamental form, the Gaussian integral evaluates the area under the bell-shaped curve described by the exponential function $e^{-x^2}$ over the entire real line.

\vspace{0.5cm}
What makes the Gaussian integral particularly remarkable is not merely its mathematical beauty but its profound utility across diverse fields. When extended to multiple dimensions, it provides essential techniques for handling complex systems with numerous degrees of freedom. The introduction of Jacobian matrices in this context allows us to navigate between different coordinate systems, preserving the physical significance of our calculations while potentially simplifying the mathematical framework.

