\section*{The Gaussian Integral in One Dimension}
The basic one-dimensional Gaussian integral is:

\[
    \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^{2}}\,dx
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
This integral cannot be evaluated using elementary antiderivatives, but it can be solved by squaring the integral and switching to polar coordinates, which brings us to multi-dimensional methods. However, one will use the spherical coordinates to approach the final result of this Gaussian integral.

\section*{Extending to Higher Dimensions} 
In three dimensions, a Gaussian function might look like:

\[
    f(x,y,z) = e^{-(x^2 + y^2 + z^2)}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
The integral of the multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ would be in a form of:

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \iiint_{\mathbb{R}^3} f(x,y,z)\,dx\,dy\,dz = \iiint_{\mathbb{R}^3} e^{-(x^2 + y^2 + z^2)}\,d(x,y,z)
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
To compute this, it is advantageous to change from Cartesian to spherical coordinates, because the exponent depends only on the radius:

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    r = \sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}
\]

\section*{Role of the Jacobian Matrix}
When changing variables in multiple integrals—such as going from Cartesian to spherical coordinates—we must adjust for how volume elements change. This is where the Jacobian matrix comes in. The transformation from Cartesian coordinates $(x, y, z)$ to spherical coordinates $(r, \theta, \varphi)$, is given by the function $\mathbf{F}: \mathbb{R}^{3} \to \mathbb{R}^{+} \times [0, \pi) \times [0, 2 \pi)$ with components

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \mathbf{F} \left(x, y, z \right)=
    \begin{cases}
        x = F_1 (r, \theta, \varphi) = r \sin{(\theta)} \cos{(\varphi)} \\
        y = F_2 (r, \theta, \varphi) = r \sin{(\theta)} \sin{(\varphi)} \\
        z = F_3 (r, \theta, \varphi) = r \cos{(\theta)}
    \end{cases}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
The Cartesian coordinate notation is $\mathbf{X}_i$, where the subscript $i$ is the number of dimention. The spherical coordinate notation (in this article) is denoted as $\mathbf{v}$ in general. A 3D spherical coordinate system uses three numbers $(r, \theta, \varphi)$ to represent a point in space, where $r \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$ is the radial distance from the origin, $\theta \in \left[ 0, \pi \right)$ is the azimuthal angle (similar to polar coordinates, akin to **latitude**), and $\varphi \in \left[ 0, 2\pi \right)$ is the polar angle (or zenith angle, akin to \textbf{longitude}). The code below is the visualization for the spherical coordinate system in \LaTeX\ language.

