\vspace{0.5cm}
The function $\mathbf{F} \left(x, y, z \right)$ represent the transformation from Cartesian coordinates $(x, y, z)$ to spherical coordinates $(r, \theta,  \varphi)$. They describe how any point in 3D space can be re-expressed in terms of radius, polar angle, and azimuthal angle. Another form on function mapping is to express them in a form of vector. 

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \mathbf{F} \left( 
        \begin{bmatrix}
        x\\
        y\\
        z
    \end{bmatrix} \right) = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        F_x (r, \theta, \varphi) \\
        F_y (r, \theta, \varphi) \\
        F_z (r, \theta, \varphi)
    \end{bmatrix} = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        r \sin{(\theta)} \cos{(\varphi)} \\
        r \sin{(\theta)} \sin{(\varphi)} \\
        r \cos{(\theta)}
    \end{bmatrix}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
Here, the vector-valued function $\mathbf{F}$ is written in matrix form to clearly show the mapping from Cartesian coordinates to spherical coordinates. This form makes it easier to construct the Jacobian matrix by taking partial derivatives of each component. 

\vspace{0.5cm}
{\large
\[
    \boldsymbol{J}_{\mathbf{F}} (r, \theta, \varphi) = 
    \left[ \frac{d(x, y, z)}{d(r,\theta,\varphi)} \right] =
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \vspace{0.25cm}
        \frac{\partial x}{\partial r} & \frac{\partial x}{\partial \theta} & \frac{\partial x}{\partial \varphi} \\
        \vspace{0.25cm}
        \frac{\partial y}{\partial r} & \frac{\partial y}{\partial \theta} & \frac{\partial y}{\partial \varphi} \\
        \frac{\partial z}{\partial r} & \frac{\partial z}{\partial \theta} & \frac{\partial z}{\partial \varphi}        
    \end{bmatrix}
\]
}

\vspace{0.5cm}
The Jacobian matrix $\boldsymbol{J}_{\mathbf{F}}$ encodes the rate of change of each Cartesian component with respect to each spherical variable. Each entry corresponds to a partial derivative $\frac{\partial \mathbf{F}_i}{\partial \mathbf{v}_j}$, and the full matrix shows how the volume element will deform under the transformation. 

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \vspace{0.5cm}           
        \frac{\partial}{\partial r} (r \sin{(\theta)} \cos{(\varphi)}) & \frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} (r \sin{(\theta)} \cos{(\varphi)}) & \frac{\partial}{\partial \varphi} (r \sin{(\theta)} \cos{(\varphi)})\\
        \vspace{0.5cm}
        \frac{\partial}{\partial r} (r \sin{(\theta)} \sin{(\varphi)}) & \frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} (r \sin{(\theta)} \sin{(\varphi)}) & \frac{\partial}{\partial \varphi} (r \sin{(\theta)} \sin{(\varphi)})\\
        \frac{\partial}{\partial r} (r \cos{(\theta)}) & \frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} (r \cos{(\theta)}) & \frac{\partial}{\partial \varphi} (r \cos{(\theta)})    
    \end{bmatrix}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
These are the computed partial derivatives based on the earlier definitions of $x,y,z$. Simplifying each expression yields a concrete Jacobian matrix filled with trigonometric and radial terms.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \begin{bmatrix}
        \vspace{0.5cm}
        \sin{(\theta)} \cos{(\varphi)} & r \cos{(\theta)} \cos{(\varphi)} & - r \sin{(\theta)} \sin{(\varphi)}\\
        \vspace{0.5cm}
        \sin{(\theta)} \sin{(\varphi)} & r \cos{(\theta)} \sin{(\varphi)} & r \sin{(\theta)} \cos{(\varphi)}\\
        \cos{(\theta)} & - r \sin{(\theta)} & 0
    \end{bmatrix}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
The Jacobian determinant tells us how to scale the volume element when switching coordinate systems.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \det (\boldsymbol{J}_{\mathbf{F}} (r, \theta, \varphi)) = r^{2} \sin{\theta}
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
After some algebraic simplification, the determinant of the Jacobian represents the scaling factor of volume during the coordinate change. For spherical coordinates, this determinant simplifies to $r^{2} \sin{\theta}$, though it may appear differently depending on formatting.

\section*{Solving the Integral}
Having established the transformation from Cartesian to spherical coordinates and derived the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we are now prepared to evaluate the Gaussian integral in three dimensions. This step marks the transition from geometrical transformation to analytical computation. The goal is to rigorously calculate the integral of the form $\iiint_{\mathbb{R}^3} e^{-(x^2 + y^2 + z^2)}\,dx\,dy\,dz$. To build a foundation for this, we begin by setting the one-dimensional Gaussian integral by $I$.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    I^{3} = \iiint_{\mathbb{R}^3} e^{-(x^2 + y^2 + z^2)}\,dx\,dy\,dz = \iiint_{\mathbb{R}^+ \times \left[0,\pi \right) \times \left[0, 2\pi \right)} e^{-r^2} (r^{2} \sin{\theta}) \,dr\,d\theta\,d\varphi 
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
Expand the integral

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \int_{0}^{\infty} r^{2} e^{-r^2}\,dr \, \int_{0}^{\pi} \sin{\theta}\,d\theta \, \int_{0}^{2\pi}\,d\varphi \,= \,    \int_{0}^{\infty} r^{2} e^{-r^2}\,dr \, \left(\cos{\theta}\right)\big]_{0}^{\pi} \, \left(\varphi\right)\big]_{0}^{2\pi} 
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
Resulting

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    I^{3} \, = \, 2\pi \int_{0}^{\infty} r^{2} e^{-r^2}\,dr
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
Now, one can substitute $u = r^{2}$, thus having $du = 2r$. Plugging in $\infty$ and $0$ into $u$ will result the 

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    I^{3} = 2\pi \int_{0}^{\infty} \sqrt{u} e^{-u} \, du
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
From the property of integral of $\int U\,dV = UV - \int V\,dU$, derived from the derivative of multiplication. Then by substituding $U = \sqrt{u}$ and $dV = e^{-u}\,dr$, will resulting in $dU = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{u}}$ and $V = -e^{-u}$. Thus, one can do the follwing substitution 

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    2\pi \left( \left. \sqrt{u}(-e^{-u}) \right]_{0}^{\infty} - \int_{0}^{\infty} -e^{-u}(\frac{1}{2\sqrt{u}}\,du) \right)
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
The acceleration of the exponential function is always faster the general polynomial one, meaning $e^{-u}$ will overtake $\sqrt{u}$. So the term of $\sqrt{u}\left.(-e^{-u}) \right]_{0}^{\infty}$ will thus be $0 - 0$. And after some mathematical rearrangement, one would obtain the following

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \pi \int_{0}^{\infty} \frac{e^{-u}}{\sqrt{u}} \, du
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
And lastly, by plugging back $u=r^2$, having $du=2r\,dr$, one will have

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \pi \int_{0}^{\infty} \frac{e^{-r^{2}}}{r}(2r)\, dr \,=\, 2\pi \int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-r^{2}} \, dr = \pi \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-r^{2}} \, dr
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
Remeber, $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-r^{2}} \, dr$ is the origional Gaussian Integral in one dimension.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    I^{3} \, = \, \pi \cdot I \longrightarrow I^2 \, = \, \pi
\]

\vspace{0.5cm}
Since variable within the integral $r$ can be any other variable, one can use $x$ as well. Ultimately, one will have

\vspace{0.5cm}
\[
    \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^{2}} \, dx \, = \, \sqrt{\pi}
\]

